# Behavioral Patterns

## Strategy

Encapsulate interchangeable algorithms behind a common interface; pick the
strategy via DI configuration, never via `if`/`switch` chains in the consumer.

```ts
export interface RetryStrategy {
  next(attempt: number): { delayMs: number; giveUp: boolean };
}

export class ExponentialBackoff implements RetryStrategy { /* ... */ }
export class FixedDelay implements RetryStrategy { /* ... */ }
```

## Observer / Domain Events

Decouple side-effects from the use case that produced them.

```ts
export interface DomainEvent {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly occurredAt: Date;
}

export class UserRegistered implements DomainEvent {
  readonly name = 'user.registered';
  readonly occurredAt = new Date();
  constructor(public readonly userId: UserId) {}
}

export interface EventBus {
  publish<E extends DomainEvent>(event: E): Promise<void>;
  subscribe<E extends DomainEvent>(
    name: E['name'],
    handler: (event: E) => Promise<void>,
  ): void;
}
```

The use case publishes `UserRegistered`; an `WelcomeEmailHandler` subscribes
in infrastructure. The use case knows nothing about email.

## Command / Use Case

Each application method is an explicit command or query object with a single
`execute` method.

```ts
export interface UseCase<TInput, TOutput> {
  execute(input: TInput): Promise<TOutput>;
}

export class RegisterUser
  implements UseCase<RegisterUserInput, Result<UserDto, UserAlreadyExists>>
{
  constructor(
    private readonly users: UserRepository,
    private readonly events: EventBus,
  ) {}

  async execute(
    input: RegisterUserInput,
  ): Promise<Result<UserDto, UserAlreadyExists>> {
    /* ... */
    return Result.ok(/* ... */);
  }
}
```

## Specification

Composable predicate objects, useful for business rules and query building.

```ts
export interface Specification<T> {
  isSatisfiedBy(c: T): boolean;
  and(other: Specification<T>): Specification<T>;
  or(other: Specification<T>): Specification<T>;
  not(): Specification<T>;
}

export abstract class BaseSpec<T> implements Specification<T> {
  abstract isSatisfiedBy(c: T): boolean;
  and(other: Specification<T>): Specification<T> { return new AndSpec(this, other); }
  or(other: Specification<T>): Specification<T> { return new OrSpec(this, other); }
  not(): Specification<T> { return new NotSpec(this); }
}

class AndSpec<T> extends BaseSpec<T> {
  constructor(private readonly a: Specification<T>, private readonly b: Specification<T>) { super(); }
  isSatisfiedBy(c: T): boolean { return this.a.isSatisfiedBy(c) && this.b.isSatisfiedBy(c); }
}
class OrSpec<T> extends BaseSpec<T> {
  constructor(private readonly a: Specification<T>, private readonly b: Specification<T>) { super(); }
  isSatisfiedBy(c: T): boolean { return this.a.isSatisfiedBy(c) || this.b.isSatisfiedBy(c); }
}
class NotSpec<T> extends BaseSpec<T> {
  constructor(private readonly s: Specification<T>) { super(); }
  isSatisfiedBy(c: T): boolean { return !this.s.isSatisfiedBy(c); }
}
```

## Chain of Responsibility

Pipeline of handlers, each can handle or pass to the next. Useful for
middleware, validation chains, approval workflows.

```ts
export interface Handler<TCtx> {
  setNext(next: Handler<TCtx>): Handler<TCtx>;
  handle(ctx: TCtx): Promise<void>;
}

export abstract class BaseHandler<TCtx> implements Handler<TCtx> {
  private next?: Handler<TCtx>;
  setNext(next: Handler<TCtx>): Handler<TCtx> { this.next = next; return next; }
  async handle(ctx: TCtx): Promise<void> {
    if (this.next) await this.next.handle(ctx);
  }
}
```

## Template Method

Use sparingly. Reserve for algorithms with a fixed skeleton and one or two
variant steps. Prefer Strategy when steps are independent.

```ts
export abstract class ImportJob<TRow> {
  async run(source: AsyncIterable<TRow>): Promise<void> {
    for await (const row of source) {
      const dto = this.parse(row);
      await this.validate(dto);
      await this.persist(dto);
    }
  }
  protected abstract parse(row: TRow): unknown;
  protected abstract validate(dto: unknown): Promise<void>;
  protected abstract persist(dto: unknown): Promise<void>;
}
```

# Creational Patterns

## Factory Method (named constructor)

Use when constructing an aggregate must enforce invariants or branch on input.

```ts
export class Order {
  private constructor(
    public readonly id: OrderId,
    private readonly lines: readonly OrderLine[],
    private status: OrderStatus,
  ) {}

  static draft(id: OrderId, lines: readonly OrderLine[]): Order {
    if (lines.length === 0) throw new EmptyOrderError(id);
    return new Order(id, lines, OrderStatus.Draft);
  }

  static rehydrate(snapshot: OrderSnapshot): Order {
    return new Order(snapshot.id, snapshot.lines, snapshot.status);
  }
}
```

Two named constructors: `draft` (new business intent) and `rehydrate` (load
from persistence) — never reuse one for the other.

## Abstract Factory

Use when a family of related objects must be produced together.

```ts
export interface ReportFactory {
  createHeader(): ReportHeader;
  createBody(): ReportBody;
  createFooter(): ReportFooter;
}

export class PdfReportFactory implements ReportFactory { /* ... */ }
export class HtmlReportFactory implements ReportFactory { /* ... */ }
```

## Builder

Use when an object has many optional, order-independent fields and you want
fluent construction in tests or configuration code.

```ts
export class HttpClientBuilder {
  private baseUrl = '';
  private timeoutMs = 5_000;
  private retries = 0;

  withBaseUrl(url: string): this { this.baseUrl = url; return this; }
  withTimeout(ms: number): this { this.timeoutMs = ms; return this; }
  withRetries(n: number): this { this.retries = n; return this; }

  build(): HttpClient {
    return new HttpClient({
      baseUrl: this.baseUrl,
      timeoutMs: this.timeoutMs,
      retries: this.retries,
    });
  }
}
```

## Singleton — only via the DI container

Do **not** write `getInstance()` singletons. Register the class in the DI
container with singleton scope; the container guarantees single instance.

```ts
container.register(Logger, { useClass: PinoLogger, lifetime: 'singleton' });
```

# Design Patterns — Complete Reference

## Factory Pattern
```typescript
export class OrderFactory {
  static createEmpty(userId: string): Order {
    return Order.createEmpty(userId);
  }

  static fromItems(userId: string, items: OrderItem[]): Order {
    const order = Order.createEmpty(userId);
    items.forEach(item => order.addItem(item));
    return order;
  }
}
```

## Repository Pattern
```typescript
// Port (application layer)
export interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, string> {
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
}

// Adapter (infrastructure)
@injectable()
export class TypeOrmUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  constructor(private dataSource: DataSource) {}

  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const entity = await this.dataSource.getRepository(UserOrmEntity).findOne({ where: { id } });
    return entity ? this.toDomain(entity) : null;
  }

  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const entity = await this.dataSource.getRepository(UserOrmEntity).findOne({ where: { email } });
    return entity ? this.toDomain(entity) : null;
  }

  async save(user: User): Promise<User> {
    const entity = this.toOrm(user);
    await this.dataSource.getRepository(UserOrmEntity).save(entity);
    return user;
  }
}
```

## Strategy Pattern
```typescript
export interface ShippingStrategy {
  calculate(items: OrderItem[]): number;
}

export class FlatRateShipping implements ShippingStrategy {
  constructor(private rate: number) {}
  calculate(): number { return this.rate; }
}

export class WeightBasedShipping implements ShippingStrategy {
  calculate(items: OrderItem[]): number {
    const weight = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.weight, 0);
    return weight * 0.5;
  }
}

// Usage
class OrderService {
  constructor(private shippingStrategy: ShippingStrategy) {}
  calculateShipping(order: Order): number {
    return this.shippingStrategy.calculate(order.items);
  }
}
```

## Adapter Pattern
```typescript
// Port
export interface PaymentService {
  charge(amount: number, token: string): Promise<{ transactionId: string }>;
}

// Adapter wrapping third-party Stripe SDK
export class StripePaymentAdapter implements PaymentService {
  constructor(private stripe: Stripe) {}

  async charge(amount: number, token: string): Promise<{ transactionId: string }> {
    const result = await this.stripe.charges.create({ amount, source: token });
    return { transactionId: result.id };
  }
}
```

## Decorator Pattern
```typescript
export interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;
}

export class ConsoleLogger implements Logger {
  log(message: string): void { console.log(message); }
}

export class JsonLoggerDecorator implements Logger {
  constructor(private decorated: Logger) {}
  log(message: string): void {
    this.decorated.log(JSON.stringify({ timestamp: new Date().toISOString(), message }));
  }
}
```

## Observer Pattern (Domain Events)
```typescript
export interface DomainEvent { type: string; payload: unknown; }

export interface EventHandler<T extends DomainEvent> {
  handle(event: T): void | Promise<void>;
}

export class EventDispatcher {
  private handlers: Map<string, EventHandler<any>[]> = new Map();

  register<T extends DomainEvent>(type: string, handler: EventHandler<T>): void {
    const list = this.handlers.get(type) || [];
    list.push(handler);
    this.handlers.set(type, list);
  }

  async dispatch(event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
    const handlers = this.handlers.get(event.type) || [];
    await Promise.all(handlers.map(h => h.handle(event)));
  }
}
```

## Command Pattern
```typescript
export interface Command<T = void> {
  execute(): Promise<T>;
}

export class CreateUserCommand implements Command<User> {
  constructor(
    private repo: UserRepository,
    private dto: CreateUserDto
  ) {}

  async execute(): Promise<User> {
    const user = User.create(this.dto.id, Email.create(this.dto.email));
    await this.repo.save(user);
    return user;
  }
}
```

## Specification Pattern
```typescript
export interface Specification<T> {
  isSatisfiedBy(candidate: T): boolean;
}

export class ActiveUserSpec implements Specification<User> {
  isSatisfiedBy(user: User): boolean {
    return user.isActive;
  }
}

export class UserWithRoleSpec implements Specification<User> {
  constructor(private role: string) {}
  isSatisfiedBy(user: User): boolean {
    return user.roles.includes(this.role);
  }
}

// Composite
export class AndSpecification<T> implements Specification<T> {
  constructor(private left: Specification<T>, private right: Specification<T>) {}
  isSatisfiedBy(candidate: T): boolean {
    return this.left.isSatisfiedBy(candidate) && this.right.isSatisfiedBy(candidate);
  }
}
```

# Structural Patterns

## Adapter

Wraps an external SDK or legacy class so the rest of the code depends on a
project-owned port.

```ts
export interface PaymentGateway {
  charge(cmd: ChargeCommand): Promise<ChargeResult>;
}

export class StripePaymentAdapter implements PaymentGateway {
  constructor(private readonly stripe: Stripe) {}

  async charge(cmd: ChargeCommand): Promise<ChargeResult> {
    const intent = await this.stripe.paymentIntents.create({
      amount: cmd.amount.cents,
      currency: cmd.amount.currency,
      payment_method: cmd.paymentMethodId,
      confirm: true,
    });
    return {
      status: intent.status === 'succeeded' ? 'succeeded' : 'failed',
      providerRef: intent.id,
    };
  }
}
```

Rule: nothing in the application layer ever imports `stripe`. They import
`PaymentGateway`.

## Decorator

Adds cross-cutting behavior (logging, caching, retries) without modifying the
decorated class.

```ts
export class CachingUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  constructor(
    private readonly inner: UserRepository,
    private readonly cache: Cache<UserId, User>,
  ) {}

  async findById(id: UserId): Promise<User | null> {
    const hit = await this.cache.get(id);
    if (hit) return hit;
    const user = await this.inner.findById(id);
    if (user) await this.cache.set(id, user);
    return user;
  }
  async save(user: User): Promise<void> {
    await this.inner.save(user);
    await this.cache.invalidate(user.id);
  }
  async delete(id: UserId): Promise<void> {
    await this.inner.delete(id);
    await this.cache.invalidate(id);
  }
}
```

Compose decorators in the DI configuration:
`Logging(Caching(SqlUserRepository))`.

## Facade

Hides a complex subsystem behind one entrypoint that the application layer
uses.

```ts
export class NotificationFacade {
  constructor(
    private readonly email: EmailSender,
    private readonly sms: SmsSender,
    private readonly push: PushSender,
    private readonly prefs: NotificationPreferences,
  ) {}

  async notify(userId: UserId, event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
    const channels = await this.prefs.channelsFor(userId, event.type);
    await Promise.all(channels.map((c) => this.dispatch(c, userId, event)));
  }

  private dispatch(/* ... */): Promise<void> { /* ... */ return Promise.resolve(); }
}
```

## Composite

Treat individual objects and groups uniformly. Useful for menu trees,
permission trees, etc.

```ts
export interface PermissionNode {
  isGranted(action: string): boolean;
}

export class Permission implements PermissionNode {
  constructor(private readonly action: string) {}
  isGranted(action: string): boolean { return this.action === action; }
}

export class PermissionGroup implements PermissionNode {
  constructor(private readonly children: readonly PermissionNode[]) {}
  isGranted(action: string): boolean {
    return this.children.some((c) => c.isGranted(action));
  }
}
```

## Proxy

Stand-in that controls access to a real subject (lazy load, access control).
Often expressed as a decorator in TypeScript; reserve "Proxy" name when the
intent is access control or lazy initialization.

# DI containers in TypeScript

## Container options

| Container | Style | Suits |
|-----------|-------|-------|
| **NestJS module graph** | Decorator-based, framework-native. | NestJS apps (default). |
| **tsyringe** | Decorator-based, lightweight, Microsoft. | Plain Node.js services. |
| **inversify** | Decorator-based, mature, plenty of features. | Larger plain Node.js codebases. |
| **awilix** | Function-based, no decorators, no `reflect-metadata`. | Teams that avoid decorators. |
| **manual** | Hand-wired in `main.ts`. | < 10 services, demo apps. |

## Provider lifetimes

| Lifetime | Meaning | Use for |
|----------|---------|---------|
| **Singleton** (default) | One instance per container. | Stateless services, repositories, ports. |
| **Transient** | New instance every resolve. | Builders, command objects with internal state. |
| **Scoped / Request** | One instance per request/scope. | Request-bound state (correlation IDs, current user) — used sparingly. |

Default to **singleton**. Document why if you escalate.

## Composition root

The single place where concrete classes are bound to interfaces. Lives
at the top level (`src/composition/`) or, in NestJS, distributes
across `*.module.ts` files but should *not* spread into use cases.

```ts
// src/composition/container.ts
import 'reflect-metadata';
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { USER_REPOSITORY } from '@users/application/ports/user-repository.js';
import { SqlUserRepository } from '@users/infrastructure/persistence/sql-user-repository.js';
import { PASSWORD_HASHER } from '@users/application/ports/password-hasher.js';
import { Argon2PasswordHasher } from '@users/infrastructure/crypto/argon2-password-hasher.js';

export function configureContainer(): void {
  container.register(USER_REPOSITORY, { useClass: SqlUserRepository });
  container.register(PASSWORD_HASHER, { useClass: Argon2PasswordHasher });
  // ... per feature
}
```

## Decorating once, in infrastructure

The application/domain layers should be **container-agnostic**. Add the
container's decorator only at the adapter:

```ts
// adapter — has @injectable
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class SqlUserRepository implements UserRepository { /* ... */ }
```

```ts
// use case — also @injectable, but only because the container needs to
// resolve constructor params; the class itself remains pure.
@injectable()
export class RegisterUser { /* ... */ }
```

If you want the use case to be 100% framework-free, register it via a
factory:

```ts
container.register(RegisterUser, {
  useFactory: (c) => new RegisterUser(
    c.resolve(USER_REPOSITORY),
    c.resolve(PASSWORD_HASHER),
    /* ... */
  ),
});
```

## Decorating without `reflect-metadata`

Awilix-style functional registration:

```ts
import { createContainer, asClass, Lifetime } from 'awilix';

const container = createContainer({ injectionMode: 'CLASSIC' });
container.register({
  userRepository: asClass(SqlUserRepository).singleton(),
  passwordHasher: asClass(Argon2PasswordHasher).singleton(),
  registerUser: asClass(RegisterUser).singleton(),
});
```

The `RegisterUser` constructor parameters are matched **by name** to
container keys.

## Don't do these

- ❌ Resolve from the container inside a use case (Service Locator).
- ❌ Singleton holding mutable per-request state.
- ❌ Two containers in the same process.
- ❌ Use the container in tests — construct the SUT directly with
  hand-rolled fakes.

## Tests don't use the container

```ts
// ✅ direct construction in unit tests
const useCase = new RegisterUser(
  new InMemoryUserRepository(),
  new FakePasswordHasher(),
  new SequentialIdGenerator(),
  new InMemoryEventBus(),
);
```

This is one of the strongest signals that your DI is correctly applied:
unit tests don't need the framework.

## Breaking circular dependencies

If module A imports module B and vice versa, extract a third module C
that owns the shared port. A and B both depend on C; C depends on
neither.

```diagram
Before:               After:
A ◀────▶ B            A ─────▶ C ◀───── B
```

